Organization = {Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc},
Abstract = {Hate speech is one type of harmful online content that attacks or
   promotes hate towards a group or individual based on their actual or
   perceived identity aspects, such as ethnicity, religion, and sexual
   orientation. It may trigger social disharmony, threaten social
   stability, and even lead to violent conflicts. Various detection
   methods, especially those based on pre-trained language models, have
   achieved outstanding results in hate speech detection tasks. Despite
   these advances, challenges remain, including the need to fine-tune
   models on specialized hate speech datasets to enhance their performance.
   Additionally, conversations on social media platforms like Telegram
   often occur in scenarios where network perception is constrained. For
   example, users often use pronouns or cite specific events to express
   slander towards certain groups, while pre-trained models often lack
   prior knowledge about these pronouns or events.
   In recent years, the powerful capabilities of large language models led
   by ChatGPT have been continuously demonstrated, especially in multiple
   tasks such as text generation and dialogue understanding. These large
   models are usually pre-trained on a large corpus, during which a large
   amount of background knowledge is stored in the model weights. Based on
   this, to address the problem of pre-trained language models in network
   perception constrained scenarios, we plan to utilize a large language
   model equipped with a comprehensive knowledge base for detecting hate
   speech. We design a zero-shot prompt and a CoT prompt to detect hate
   speech. We propose a Chinese hate speech dataset comprising about
   200,000 data points. Our methods prove to be effective across two
   datasets.<bold> </bold>},
